  "final_text": "The dashboard and the alert rule both exist in Kibana now. I haven't seen either one run yet: the panels haven't been rendered and the rule's status is still \"pending\".\n\n**1. What the data holds.** The data view `kibana_sample_data_logs` uses `timestamp` as its time field. Each document is one web request with:\n- the visitor's IP address (`clientip`)\n- the response code (`response`, stored as text, not a number)\n- the requested `url` and `request`\n- how many `bytes` were sent\n- the browser (`agent`), the visitor's operating system (`machine.os`) and the file `extension`\n- location: source and destination country and coordinates (`geo.*`)\n- `referer` and `tags`\n\n**2. Dashboard \"EFF-70422ab6 Web logs overview\"** has 7 panels: http://localhost:15601/app/dashboards#/view/eff-70422ab6-web-logs-overview-006682766798\n- Three single numbers: total requests, unique visitors (counted by IP address) and total bytes served\n- Requests over time, split by response code (stacked bar)\n- Share of each response code (pie)\n- Top 10 URLs by request count, with the average bytes for each (table)\n- Top 10 source countries by request count (bar)\n\n**3. Rule \"EFF-70422ab6 5xx spike\"** (id `3c6a665a-41e9-4a12-bb89-a7fdfa538f40`) is an Elasticsearch query rule on `kibana_sample_data_logs`. Every minute it counts requests with a response code from 500 to 599 in the last 5 minutes, using `timestamp`. It fires when that count is more than 10.\n- **It's already switched on.** I enabled it when I created it; by default it would have started off.\n- **It has no actions,** so when it fires it only records an alert in Kibana. It won't notify anyone. If you want a Slack, email or other message, a connector has to be added.\n- **The 5xx check compares response codes as text.** That works for three-digit codes, but I couldn't confirm the actual values in the index because Kibana's console proxy is turned off here.",
